    49	## 6. PB — the C1P "runner discrepancy" (Priority B)
    50	
    51	| record | built first / second | wall (min-of-medians, ms) | graph breaks | Δ first − second |
    52	|---|---|---|---|---|
    53	| standalone unprofiled C1P r1/r2/r3 (r1 SDR PASS, +0.0002 dB) | — | 36.377 / 36.358 / 36.797 (HEADLINE-grade cell, clock-clean) | 8 | — |
    54	| matched C1 vs C1P m1–m3 | C1 / C1P | 36.78–36.83 / 52.04–52.05 | 8 / 5 | **−15.259** [−15.299, −15.219] |
    55	| matched C1P vs C1 m1–m2 (order swapped) | C1P / C1 | 36.91, 36.79 / 52.05, 52.00 | 8 / 5 | **−15.220** [−15.291, −15.167] |
    56	| standalone traced C1P / C1 (same session) | — | block medians 36.98 / 37.01 | 8 / 8 | — |
    57	
    58	Direct observation: the slow configuration follows the build position, not the arm. Standalone unprofiled C1P runs at 36.36–36.80 ms (8 breaks, NOT_FULLY_GRAPHED as always for this family), in line with this session's traced runs (C1 37.01 ms, C1P 36.98 ms under the profiler, caches disabled) — a same-session unprofiled C1 baseline was not part of the PB design, so "C1P equals C1 standalone" is stated only for the traced pair; whichever of C1/C1P is compiled second in a matched process compiles with 5 breaks instead of 8 and runs ≈ 15.2 ms slower. The Phase-3 statement "C1P is 15 ms slower than C1" is withdrawn as an arm property and restated as a matched-runner artefact for arms that compile with graph breaks (the swapped order makes C1 the slow arm); the Phase-3 records are unchanged and re-labelled ("compiled C1 vs partially compiled C1P"). C3-family arms show no such effect (A/A −0.009 ms at 8 s; identical break counts in both positions).
    59	
    60	Composition from the profiled matched process (repeat 0 of both arms in one nsys capture, host NVTX windows, 50 passes each; `traces/matched_trace_C1_C1P_352800_p1.json`): first-built C1 wall 37.45 ms, GPU busy 34.70, GPU span 37.12, 1,354 kernels, 96 graph launches; second-built C1P wall 52.89 ms, GPU busy 49.34, GPU span 52.49, 1,569 kernels, 51 graph launches. The 15.4 ms wall difference is 14.6 ms of additional GPU-busy time (215 more kernels per pass launched as fewer, larger graphs); GPU idle inside the window is 2.76 vs 3.52 ms. No span subtraction was used; the "all host-side" reading is refuted. Profiler perturbation on these blocks: 0.02 ms (C1) and 0.24 ms (C1P) relative to the same record's unprofiled repeats.
    61	
    62	Mechanism, supported by the torch logs of this session in both build orders and absent from every standalone run: the two model instances share code objects, so the second build re-enters the first build's dynamo frames and adds guard-failure recompiles instead of tracing fresh frames; every C1-family build exhausts the recompile limit on the einops `rearrange` frame (`[3/8] torch._dynamo hit config.recompile_limit (8)`; part of the known 8-break structure), and only the second build in a matched process also exhausts it on the model's top-level `forward` frame (`[7/8] torch._dynamo hit config.recompile_limit (8)`, function `forward` at mel_band_roformer.py:127, last reason "tensor 'x' size mismatch at index 0. expected 60, actual 801"; present in `torchlogs_matched_C1_C1P_352800_m*.log` and `torchlogs_matched_C1P_C1_352800_m*.log`, absent from `torchlogs_C1P_352800_r*.log` and the standalone trace logs), after which dynamo stops compiling that frame: the top-level forward of the second-built model runs eager and only the frames it calls are compiled as they are reached, which is a different graph/break structure. That is consistent with the profiled composition (more, smaller kernels; fewer graph launches; more GPU time) and for the 5-vs-8 break count. The link limit → eager fallback → 15 ms is torch's documented behaviour and is recorded, but it was not tested by intervention (raising `torch._dynamo.config.recompile_limit` for the second build); that intervention is the smallest follow-up if the matched runner is ever used again for graph-breaking arms. *Stage-2 addendum (after this report; engineering host, not the registered protocol): the intervention was run on ml-beast — raising `recompile_limit` to 64 in the same C1 vs C1P process removes the artefact (−14.75 → −0.22 ms, no limit hit); `docs/phase4/stage2/PILOT_RESULTS.md`.* Standalone unprofiled C1 was not part of the registered PB design (C1 was measured standalone in sessions 1–3); the result generator lists it as absent.
