docs/related_work/README.md:43:**Graph deployment and execution optimization (F1, F2, F5, F6, F7).** The effect this study measures is established: compiled and graphed inference is faster than eager by the documented 1.5–2.3×, graph breaks cost real time (GraphMend), per-replay copies are a known first-order cost (PyGraph; NVIDIA), and one full graph beats segmented and piecewise variants (SGLang). What was not found in the sources reviewed is the accounting: a per-pass decomposition of a reduce-overhead forward into staging copies, lost fusion, idle between graph launches and kernel selection, a cause class made of incidental Python rather than control flow, per-node stream, graph and PDL-graph costs on SM120, and a priced autocast re-cast term that moves from 3 % to 37 % between operating points. The megakernel literature (Cohere, MPK, Hazy) sets the terms for F5c and F6: the study's X12 is a boundary-removal figure with the excluded mechanisms enumerated, and F6 is a statement about when boundary removal pays, with achieved bandwidth named as the other lever.
docs/related_work/README.md:47:**Band-split workloads and ragged GEMMs (F3, F4, F8, SM120).** Grouped-GEMM work is ragged along M with fixed K; MelBandRoformer's band split is ragged along K with fixed M, and torch 2.8.0 gates the grouped path to compute capability 9.0 while its 2D×2D mode would express ragged K. The pad-and-batch result is therefore a measured comparison in this shape regime (−1.7 % at 8 s, +40 % at 100 ms, with the decomposition into batching, padding, efficiency and non-GEMM terms), not an absence claim. On SM120 itself, Jarmusch et al. establish the instruction set (no wgmma, no tcgen05 on GB203); the study's characterization is at the framework level (realized rates by accumulator through cuBLAS/Triton under PyTorch 2.8, SDPA backend behaviour, feature gating, launch/PDL slopes) and is stated as not found in the sources reviewed for the RTX 5090.
