# Chen, "Memory-Bound but Not Bandwidth-Limited" (arXiv 2605.30571v1, May 2026; not peer-reviewed)

**Reading-order item 9.** Tag: F5, F6, ledger, methodology OVERLAP.

## Research question
When a decode step is memory-bound by arithmetic intensity, is it actually bandwidth-limited in practice, and does a preregistered CUDA-graph A/B recover the gap?

## Main contribution (authors' claims, as read)
An analytic floor t_floor = (W + K) / B_peak (weights plus KV bytes over peak bandwidth), a ratio R_floor of floor to measured time, and a preregistered CUDA-graph A/B with kill criteria and confidence intervals. Results: CUDA graphs give 1.259× (95 % CI [1.253, 1.267], N = 10) on H100 versus 1.028× on L4; R_floor 0.27 on H100 versus 0.81 on L4, read as launch-side overhead dominating on the faster GPU. 44 cells in the study.

## Evidence and scope
LLM decode, two GPUs (H100, L4), preregistered protocol with CIs; single author; arXiv only.

## Exact version and date read
arXiv 2605.30571v1 HTML; read 2026-09-20.

## Relevant source locations
Floor definition; R_floor table; preregistration and kill criteria; CUDA-graph A/B table.

## Relationship to this study
- Overlap: nearest design cousin. Preregistered predictions with kill rules, an analytic floor, a CUDA-graph A/B with intervals. The study's registration lives in `prereg_amendments.md` (commit e523ef9, pushed before session 1; `docs/provenance/push_evidence.md`) and its intervals in `docs/phase3/stats/bootstrap_headlines.csv` and `bootstrap_pairs.csv` (10,000 block-level resamples).
- Difference: Chen's floor is a bytes-over-bandwidth point estimate for the whole step; the ledger is per-class and per-mechanism with confidence grades. Chen measures the same GPU class as Hazy; this study measures SM120.
- Scientifically meaningful: the R_floor idea supports F6's boundary-removal wording (launch-side terms matter when the floor is small); it is not the study's.

## Consequence for this work
Cite Chen for the preregistered-A/B form and for R_floor; describe the study's registration as self-registered with public time evidence, never as a registered report.

## Unresolved
Chen's exact N per cell beyond the H100 A/B; peer-review status.
# Abdelfattah, Haidar, Tomov & Dongarra, "Performance, Design, and Autotuning of Batched GEMM for GPUs" (ISC High Performance 2016)

**Contribution-test source (closest prior on batched/padded versus per-problem GEMM execution as a function of size).** Read from the ICL PDF (`icl-utk-871-2016.pdf`, `pdftotext` extraction `scratchpad/abdelfattah16.txt`) on 2026-09-22.

## Research question
How should a GEMM kernel for batches of small matrices, of fixed or variable size, be designed and tuned on a GPU, and when does it beat the alternatives?

## Main contribution (authors' claims)
Batched GEMM kernels for fixed-size and variable-size ("vbatched") batches in MAGMA, built from templated "ETM" kernels with an autotuning process over tile shapes, plus a dynamic-parallelism variant that launches one GEMM per matrix from the GPU side. Abstract: "We address batched problems with both fixed and variable sizes, and show that specialized GEMM designs and a comprehensive autotuning process are needed to handle problems of small sizes."

## Evidence and scope (authors' claims)
Tesla K40c (745 MHz, ECC on), double precision, §4 "Performance Results and Analysis". Competitors: cuBLAS batched GEMM, "the cuBLAS classic GEMM kernel offloaded to concurrent streams" (the per-problem alternative), and MKL. Size-dependent winners are stated, not modelled: for variable sizes, "The two MAGMA kernels outperform other competitors for Maximum m up to 300. The streamed GEMM dominates the midrange, and then gets nearly matched or slightly outperformed by the MAGMA kernel based on dynamic parallelism. For the case of square matrices (Figures 11(f) and 12(f)), the streamed GEMM achieves the best performance unless matrices are too small, where the ETM-based MAGMA kernel is the best choice." For fixed sizes: "Otherwise, the streamed cuBLAS kernel mostly achieves the best performance, expect for the midrange in Figure 9(e), where MAGMA takes over." Sub-warp sizes: "Performance there is memory bound and can be modeled."

## Exact version and date read
ISC 2016 paper PDF as hosted by ICL (icl-utk-871-2016), read 2026-09-22.

## Relevant source locations
§3 (design, vbatched variants, dynamic parallelism), §4 (Figures 9, 11, 12 and the quoted winner-by-size statements), §5.

## Relationship to this study
- Overlap: the same question as E9 in a different setting: when does batching many small (variable-size) GEMMs beat running them one by one, as a function of size. Their variable dimension is the matrix size within a batch; this study's is the chunk length (frames), with the 60 band GEMMs ragged along K.
- Difference: their answer is empirical per GPU and per shape class (tile autotuning, no cost model with fixed per-call terms); their crossover is between a batched kernel and streamed individual cuBLAS calls at the kernel level, inside a numerical library, on a K40c. This study asks the same kind of question at the application level (pad-and-batch versus per-band GEMMs inside a compiled, graphed forward), registers an attributed cost model with fixed and length-scaling terms, and tests it at held-out lengths.
- Scientifically meaningful difference: yes in method (registered model, held-out test) and in level (whole forward under CUDA graphs); no in the existence of a size-dependent crossover, which they establish for batched versus streamed GEMM.

## Consequence for this work
The contribution test cites this paper for "batched versus per-problem GEMM crossovers exist and are size-dependent"; the study's claim is restricted to the attributed model, its held-out failure, and the direction and bracket of the crossover for this workload class.

## Unresolved
Whether the authors' later vbatched paper (ICS 2017, "Novel HPC techniques to batch execution of many variable size BLAS computations on GPUs") adds a cost-model rule; its full text was not opened (ACM DL).
# NVIDIA, "CUDA Graph Best Practice for PyTorch" — Troubleshooting: Performance Issues, and Torch Integration

**Reading-order item 2.** Tag: F2, F7 THREAT/OVERLAP.

## Research question
Not a study. Vendor guidance: what makes CUDA-graph deployment of PyTorch models slow, and how the torch integration (manual capture, `torch.compile` cudagraph trees) behaves.

## Main contribution (authors' claims, as read)
Named failure modes with prescribed remedies. The pages read name, in their own headings and sentences: "Too Many Small CUDA Graphs" (fragmentation of one forward into many graphs, each paying launch and serialization cost); input-copy overhead at every replay when inputs are not written into the static buffers directly; channel serialization when graphs are launched back to back; and, for autocast, that a "cast operation is part of the captured graph" and the advice to "Always Disable Cache with CUDA Graphs" (the autocast weight-cast cache is unsafe with capture).

## Evidence and scope
Prose and small code examples; no measurements, no models, no GPU named for a number. Execution mode: manual `torch.cuda.CUDAGraph` capture and `torch.compile` with cudagraph trees.

## Exact version and date read
docs.nvidia.com/dl-cuda-graph, "latest" (the pages carry no version string or date in the body read). Read on 2026-09-20 (`troubleshooting/performance-issues.html`, `torch-cuda-graph/torch-integration.html`).

## Relevant source locations
Performance-issues page: sections on graph fragmentation, input copies, serialization. Torch-integration page: the autocast section.

## Relationship to this study
- Overlap: the mechanisms the pages describe are exactly the ones the C1 trace shows (many graphs per pass, staging copies per replay, idle between graphs) and the one X6a prices (casts captured inside the graph and replayed).
- Difference: the pages describe; they attach no number. This study attaches per-pass numbers under one model: 96 graph launches, 63 staging copies, 0.735 ms idle and 2.502 ms of copies per 8 s pass for C1 (`docs/phase3/x1/x1_decomposition_352800.json`); weight re-casts 0.9198 ms at 8 s and 0.8574 ms at 100 ms for C3 (`LEDGER.md` X6a).
- On the cast condition: the pages say the cache must be disabled under capture; the study's determination is that the compiled arms carry the casts as `aten._to_copy` graph operations on parameters that have `requires_grad=True` (672 of 674), replayed by cudagraph trees, and that eager hits the C++ weight-cast cache within a block (`docs/phase3/00_first_message.md` addendum; `docs/phase3/probes/autocast_probe.out`). That is consistent with the pages and adds the condition.
- Scientifically meaningful difference: the pricing and the operating-point dependence (3.19 % of C3 at 8 s versus 36.66 % at 100 ms) are not in the vendor text.

## Consequence for this work
F2 and F7 cite these pages for the mechanism and claim only the attribution. The word "negligible" for re-casts is never used without noting the operating point at which it stops being true.

## Unresolved
Page version and date (no version string found); whether the pages have changed since 2026-09-20.

