docs/codex/13_stage2.log:826:`torch.compile` workloads with graph breaks and shape/dtype recompiles where more than one instance of a module class is compiled per process; the artefact scales with the fraction of the graph that falls back; it vanishes for break-free graphs, with isolated caches, or with separate processes.
docs/codex/13_stage2.log:829:- PyTorch user guide, "Dealing with Recompilations" (docs main, 2026): the limit (`recompile_limit`, alias `cache_size_limit`, default 8; accumulated limit) and the consequence — "all future compilation attempts will result in the function being skipped (run eagerly)", "all nested function calls WILL be skipped (Dynamo will try to use previously compiled bytecode for the nested functions)".
docs/codex/13_stage2.log:830:- PyTorch DevLog, "Dynamo Isolate Recompiles" (2026-05-04): names the problem exactly — "multiple `torch.compile()` calls targeting the same underlying code object share a single cache and recompile limit" — and ships `torch.compile(isolate_recompiles=True)` (post-2.8 versions; not in the pinned 2.8.0).
docs/codex/13_stage2.log:832:- What prior work establishes: the sharing and the eager fallback, and an upstream fix. **What this study establishes:** a quantified ranking inversion (41 %) in a controlled matched harness, its log/trace signature, and that it hides inside "valid" records unless per-frame counters are read. **What remains:** whether a bounded diagnostic (recompile-limit hits + break/kernel/launch-count equality between standalone and in-context builds) is sufficient in general — a modest question.
docs/codex/13_stage2.log:838:Pilot A1 (done): the same matched process with `recompile_limit` 8 vs 64. Predicted under the mechanism: no `[7/8]` hit and block medians within ≈ 0.5 ms at 64. Observed: A/A C1 process — no hit and no gap at either limit (identical options reuse entries); C1 vs C1P — −14.75 ms with the hit at 8, −0.22 ms without any hit at 64. Follow-up when a torch build with `isolate_recompiles=True` is pinned: the same pair with isolation.
docs/codex/13_stage2.log:844:A1 removed the artefact: the outcome is *already explained by prior work* (documented sharing and eager fallback; upstream `isolate_recompiles`) — keep as a rule: separate processes, or a raised/isolated recompile budget, for arms that compile with graph breaks; publish per-frame counters and standalone/in-context equality checks with every matched record. No research program follows from it.
