docs/codex/13_stage2.log:1144:Mechanism, supported by the torch logs of this session in both build orders and absent from every standalone run: the two model instances share code objects, so the second build re-enters the first build's dynamo frames and adds guard-failure recompiles instead of tracing fresh frames; every C1-family build exhausts the recompile limit on the einops `rearrange` frame (`[3/8] torch._dynamo hit config.recompile_limit (8)`; part of the known 8-break structure), and only the second build in a matched process also exhausts it on the model's top-level `forward` frame (`[7/8] torch._dynamo hit config.recompile_limit (8)`, function `forward` at mel_band_roformer.py:127, last reason "tensor 'x' size mismatch at index 0. expected 60, actual 801"; present in `torchlogs_matched_C1_C1P_352800_m*.log` and `torchlogs_matched_C1P_C1_352800_m*.log`, absent from `torchlogs_C1P_352800_r*.log` and the standalone trace logs), after which dynamo stops compiling that frame: the top-level forward of the second-built model runs eager and only the frames it calls are compiled as they are reached, which is a different graph/break structure. That is consistent with the profiled composition (more, smaller kernels; fewer graph launches; more GPU time) and for the 5-vs-8 break count. The link limit → eager fallback → 15 ms is torch's documented behaviour and is recorded, but it was not tested by intervention (raising `torch._dynamo.config.recompile_limit` for the second build); that intervention is the smallest follow-up if the matched runner is ever used again for graph-breaking arms. *Stage-2 addendum (after this report; engineering host, not the registered protocol): the intervention was run on ml-beast — raising `recompile_limit` to 64 in the same C1 vs C1P process removes the artefact (−14.75 → −0.22 ms, no limit hit); `docs/phase4/stage2/PILOT_RESULTS.md`.* Standalone unprofiled C1 was not part of the registered PB design (C1 was measured standalone in sessions 1–3); the result generator lists it as absent.
docs/codex/13_stage2.log:1219:**Explanations supported by discriminating evidence:** the second-built arm's slow-down is GPU-busy time after dynamo's recompile limit is exhausted on the model's top-level forward frame in the second build (logs in both orders, never standalone; profiled composition). Freezing's gain comes with the removal of the 420 parameter casts, rotary folding, norm+cast shrinkage and 413 fewer launches, and against a GEMM-class slow-down that is largest at 100 ms (−0.37 ms), a possible contributor to the smaller 100 ms gain on this base (the other two bases have no session-4 control trace, so the cross-base difference is not explained).
docs/codex/13_stage2.log:1221:**Plausible but unverified:** that the launch-count reduction accounts for the gap between device-time deltas and wall gains (not testable with the available per-node figures, which include kernel execution); that raising the recompile limit would remove the matched-runner artefact; that freezing's shift of GEMMs into Triton templates on the max-autotune base is what makes C3MG the fastest configuration (23.56 ms at 8 s, cross-session template counts).
