   115	### E. Closest prior work
   116	Abdelfattah et al., batched/variable-size GEMM (ISC 2016; note 14); MegaBlocks and TMA-adaptive grouped GEMM (padding cost 1.7–20.4 % on H800; F3); cuBLAS grouped GEMM (1.2× over looping); llama.cpp PR #27637 kernel choice by batch size (note 15); TensorRT optimization profiles (note 16); Chen's R_floor (note 9) and the launch-floor literature (F5). Prior work establishes ragged-M batching costs and floors; **this study establishes** a length-dependent flip between two ragged-K strategies on one GPU with intervals, a failed affine predictor, and the per-frame efficiency curves. **Remains:** a mechanism-level predictor validated prospectively on a new layout, and whether an sm120 ragged-K grouped kernel would dominate both.
   117	
   118	### F. Potential contribution
   119	A decision rule for deployment (strategy by chunk length and band layout) and a validated per-group latency-floor model; a boundary for when building grouped ragged-K kernels is worth it. Users: audio-separation deployers, band-split model authors, kernel authors choosing what to build. Domain-specific, but the modelling question (floors vs waste in ragged groups) is broader.
   120	
   121	### G. Smallest discriminating experiment
   122	Matched C3MG vs HG at 100 ms, 1 s, 4 s, 6 s, 8 s (registered protocol) with node traces, plus the per-kernel band-projection GEMM comparison (selection vs waste). Prediction under 1: the crossover persists near 6–8 s and the band-projection GEMM kernels are identical between C3MG and HG with HG's ≈ 3.9× longer at 8 s; under 3: different kernels/configs at 8 s. A synthetic equal-width layout (waste 1.0) as a structural variant (the Phase-3 E5 variant machinery exists) predicts no crossover under 1.
   123	
   124	### H. Transfer or boundary test
   125	BS-RoFormer's layout (different dispersion) — new data; a GPU with a different per-launch floor (H100-class) shifts the crossover in a predicted direction; the equal-width variant is the boundary case.
   126	
   127	### I. Decision criteria
   128	Supports: the floor model predicts the C3MG/HG crossover within the declared threshold on a held-out length and the equal-width variant removes it. Weakens: crossover explained by kernel selection (3). Rejects: no crossover after optimization or the floor model mispredicts by more than the A/A spread. Labels as in Candidate 1; the E9 failure is not refitted — a new prospective registration is required.
   145	## Limitations
   146	Pilot probes ran on a shared engineering host with coarse event timing and idle-clock behaviour between iterations; they are directional. The Stage-1 evidence is one checkpoint, one model family, RTX 5090s only; the trace attribution is descriptive (node-level, caches disabled). The prior-work search used web search and the Phase-3 notes; access to some venues' full texts was not attempted, and an unsuccessful search is not proof of absence.
   147	
   148	## Recommendation
   149	Candidate 1 is recommended for deeper investigation: it is mechanistic, cheap to test, already supported by matched evidence on two GPUs and by an isolated pilot that separates warming from generic effects, and it generalizes beyond this model to any producer→consumer operand pattern in latency-bound inference (casts, dequantization, conversions). Candidate 3 is the better domain result but costs a pod session per step and a second checkpoint; Candidate 2 is a rule.
   150	
   151	**The most promising next research question is whether the loss of producer-side operand warming (L2 or translation residency) predictably offsets the gain from removing redundant cast/dequant kernels in latency-bound inference, because the corrected evidence shows unchanged GEMM kernels slowing by ≈ 40 % of the removed cast time at the 100 ms point on two GPUs and the isolated pilot reproduces it with the warming signature. The smallest experiment that would materially change this assessment is the matched C3G vs C3G-plus-weight-touch pair at 100 ms under the registered protocol (with an unrelated-touch control and a sparse-touch arm): no recovery would show the pilot does not transfer to graph replay, and equal recovery from the unrelated touch would reduce the effect to a generic preceding-kernel artefact.**
     5	## Probe A1 — intervention on the matched-runner artefact (candidate A)
     6	
     7	- **Question.** Is the 15 ms slow-down of the second-built C1-family arm caused by dynamo's recompile limit being exhausted on the model's top-level `forward` frame (shared code object), so that raising the limit removes it?
     8	- **Design.** Matched A/A process `matched_run.py --pair C1 C1 --chunk 352800 --repeats 2 --no-manifest` twice: (i) default `torch._dynamo.config.recompile_limit = 8`; (ii) `recompile_limit = 64` set before any compile (wrapper `probe_a1_recompile_limit.py`). Read from the torch logs: presence of `[7/8] … hit config.recompile_limit` in the second build, graph-break counts of both builds, recorded cudagraphs, block medians of both arms.
